00:00:04.422 --> 00:00:06.130
CHRIS LATIMER: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.336
My name is Chris Latimer.

00:00:07.336 --> 00:00:08.460
Thanks for joining us here.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.668
I know that we're the only
thing standing between you

00:00:10.668 --> 00:00:12.570
and drinks at the
cocktail reception,

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.180
so we definitely appreciate
your attendance for our session

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.061
here, today, on securing the
enterprise with secure APIs.

00:00:19.061 --> 00:00:20.610
I just now realized that rhymed.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.019
I never actually said
it out loud before.

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:24.060
My name is Chris Latimer.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.950
I'm one of the field
engineers from Apigee,

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.700
now part of Google, and
I'll let my cohorts here

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.440
introduce themselves
later on, when

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.634
they get to their part of
the presentation as well.

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:35.550
Really excited to be
here and talk to you guys

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.230
today about this topic.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.440
Being Apigee, we
absolutely love APIs.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.660
And so what do we
mean when we say

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.220
APIs, when we talk about
APIs and API security today?

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.430
Are we just talking about
the technical aspects of API,

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.730
what protocols we're using,
system to system integration--

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.080
that type of thing?

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:55.800
The answer is no.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.730
We really think about
APIs more broadly.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.390
We think about them from
a technical perspective,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.360
but we also think about them
from a business perspective.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.917
And later on, we'll get to
hear from Ticketmaster and some

00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:07.500
of the interesting
things that they're

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.270
doing from an API
perspective and how

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.120
they're securing their APIs.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.270
So if you look at what all
we're doing with APIs today,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.190
it's hard to go even
more than five minutes

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.870
without using an
API of some form.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.642
If you check social media,
you're using an API.

00:01:21.642 --> 00:01:23.850
When you checked into your
flight, checked the flight

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.855
status, that's another API call.

00:01:25.855 --> 00:01:27.480
When you make a
purchase online, that's

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.020
also going to be
another API call,

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.959
so of course, we need
to secure these APIs.

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:33.750
Very, very important
data and functionality

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.710
are running through
APIs, so we often

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.910
want to make sure that
we're going to secure those,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.340
and our first
reaction is to think

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.290
about something like this.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.774
We're thinking about, well,
that's a lot of valuable data

00:01:45.774 --> 00:01:47.190
that somebody could
get access to,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.207
that maybe they
shouldn't get access to,

00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:50.790
and so what we're
going to do is we're

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.516
going to lock this down
as tightly as possible.

00:01:53.516 --> 00:01:55.890
Put everything in the vault.
Don't let anybody get access

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.060
to this stuff.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.010
Put in place all the
monitoring, all the tools

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:03.690
that we can to really lock
this important capability down.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.200
And the problem with that
is that, if all of your API

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.699
security starts
to look like this,

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:15.120
you're missing out on a lot of
the value that APIs can bring.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.171
So the hard part of
API security isn't just

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.170
locking everything down.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.795
If we just wanted to
have a secure API,

00:02:20.795 --> 00:02:22.530
we could just not
build any APIs.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.780
That's the most
secure API of all.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.410
But putting together a
secure API while still

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:33.390
being able to foster innovation
inside of your organization,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.480
avoid putting in place
unnecessary delays,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.400
red tape, bureaucracy--

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.190
doing all that while
keeping your API secure

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.750
is a much, much harder
problem to solve.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.290
And so how do we do that?

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.035
And in order to think
about this problem,

00:02:52.035 --> 00:02:54.410
I like to put it into the
context of imagine that you are

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.380
just creating a new account
online-- you see a new site;

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.180
you want to register
for an account--

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.670
and the way that we would
think about this process.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.620
If the site wants your
email, sure, why not?

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.720
I give this out all
the time to any site.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.400
Its pretty usual
that they want that.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.610
They want my address.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.860
Not a big deal to give
that information out.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.152
It's usually pretty
easy to find.

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:16.235
You start to get to something
like a phone number.

00:03:16.235 --> 00:03:18.700
Well, now, I'm a
little skeptical.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.060
Am I going to get robocalls?

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.580
Are you going to text me?

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.080
Is this for two-factor auth?

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.619
Is there a good reason for it?

00:03:24.619 --> 00:03:26.910
And if they say something
like, oh, what's your social?

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.140
Well, now, you're going
to slam the brakes on.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.077
Almost nothing is going to get
access to that information.

00:03:33.077 --> 00:03:34.660
You might think about
it a little bit.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.340
Is there a legitimate
need for me

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.460
to give this information out?

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.530
There might be in certain cases.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.100
You'll look and see, is
this actually the site

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.262
that it claims to be, or
is this a broken SSL cert?

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:52.220
And if you're comfortable
with the risk reward,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.230
you're comfortable with
handing out that information.

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:59.380
An API security requires
a similar approach.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.420
We're going to expose
different types of APIs,

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.628
and they're going to have
different types of security

00:04:03.628 --> 00:04:06.750
requirements that we're going
to want to impose on those APIs.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.095
So let's look through
a few examples here.

00:04:09.095 --> 00:04:11.220
Let's imagine that you're
working for a health care

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.104
company, maybe a
hospital, and you

00:04:13.104 --> 00:04:14.520
want to create an
API that's going

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.180
to expose hospital locations
and hours, public information.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.094
There's nothing
sensitive about this,

00:04:20.094 --> 00:04:21.510
so we can just
open it up and just

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.080
let everybody go use
this thing like crazy.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.600
Well, in a sense, yes,
but there are still things

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.490
we need to be concerned about.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.790
For example, this may be part of
a mission critical application

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:33.600
for us.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.605
This may be what--

00:04:35.605 --> 00:04:37.230
you know when you
call up the hospital,

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.354
and you ask where the
nearest location is, somebody

00:04:39.354 --> 00:04:40.904
might be using the same API.

00:04:40.904 --> 00:04:43.320
When you go to the website,
you may be using the same API.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.897
So if all of a sudden,
you open this up

00:04:45.897 --> 00:04:48.230
and somebody starts sending
a flood of traffic your way,

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.500
that can still cause pretty
major problems for you.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.902
So we need to think about that.

00:04:53.902 --> 00:04:55.610
The traditional approach
to this is let's

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.500
put in place
heavyweight governance.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:58.520
Let's lock this down.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.170
Let's not give anybody
access unless we

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.650
go through a rigorous process
to understand how you're

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.400
going to be using this
API, how many calls

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.230
are you going to send to this.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.360
But a better approach to
that is traffic management,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.710
so we can put in place things
like quotas and spike arrest

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.230
and so forth to
limit our exposure

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.870
while still maintaining
that culture of fostering

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.252
innovation.

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:21.710
Another thing that
we'll usually do

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.870
is try to put into place
a service registry.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.600
Let me figure out exactly
who's using which API

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.220
and make sure that we're
tracking this information very,

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:30.860
very closely.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.850
A better approach of that is to
use something like an API key,

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:35.484
where we can do
this automatically.

00:05:35.484 --> 00:05:37.400
So rather than have a
registry that's probably

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.370
going to get out of date and out
of sync and give us unreliable

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:43.060
information, what we can
do is we can use an API

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:47.000
key to automatically make sure
that each application that's

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.947
making an API call is
authorized to do so.

00:05:50.947 --> 00:05:53.030
We also have to worry about
content based attacks.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.890
Even though this API may not
expose sensitive information

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.870
on purpose, if
somebody starts sending

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.579
in SQL injections or
cross-site scripting

00:06:01.579 --> 00:06:03.286
or those type of
things, we have to still

00:06:03.286 --> 00:06:05.450
be cognizant of the
payload and make sure we're

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.650
validating that payload.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.290
And a common way of
approaching this problem

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.135
is with an API gateway, and
because I came from Apigee,

00:06:13.135 --> 00:06:14.760
I'm going to happen
to mention, we just

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.577
have a really good
API gateway that

00:06:16.577 --> 00:06:18.410
solves a lot of these
problems that you have

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.890
that we're talking about today.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.680
Now, I promise this is not going
to be a commercial for Apigee,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.180
but I did just want
to mention this.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.846
So if you want to
talk more about Apigee

00:06:25.846 --> 00:06:29.136
and its capabilities,
we are happy to do so.

00:06:29.136 --> 00:06:30.510
So let's think
about another API.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.580
Let's think about one
that, all of a sudden,

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.552
is opening up scheduling
or doctor availability.

00:06:36.552 --> 00:06:38.010
So now, we've got
a few more things

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.260
that we have to think about.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.690
We have to start thinking about
we don't want anybody just

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:45.090
registering for
access to this API

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.210
and starting to create
appointments for doctors.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.670
We want to have a
process in place that

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.970
lets us vet the partners who
are going to be using this API,

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.470
and the traditional
approach to this

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.550
is to have a manual process,
the same type of thing.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.700
We're going to have a form that
somebody's going to fill out.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.241
We're going to take
this information.

00:07:01.241 --> 00:07:04.514
We're going to decide whether or
not you get access to this API.

00:07:04.514 --> 00:07:05.930
A better approach
is self-service,

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.430
so rather than you answer a
bunch of questions about how

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.017
your API works, instead of
you looking through and having

00:07:12.017 --> 00:07:14.100
conversations and meetings
and slowing things down

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.800
and coordinating schedules,
we can have a developer portal

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.710
that's going to give you the
ability to give your partner

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.300
self-service aspects--

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.470
self-service capabilities to
register for access to an API,

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:30.600
see the documentation, and
start to use it right away.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.900
We are also going to need
things authentications,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:33.780
so we're going to need identity.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.113
We're going to need to make
sure that the person that is

00:07:36.113 --> 00:07:37.590
registering for a--

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.270
or that is scheduling
an appointment

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.860
is the person that's the
patient in our system.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.689
So we need some way
of identifying folks,

00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:46.980
and the traditional approach
is going to be direct user

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:47.640
authentication.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.250
If I have partners who are
going to be authenticating

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:54.000
my patients, I don't necessarily
want them to have access

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.670
to that patient's credentials.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:00.780
Instead, a better approach is to
use delegated identity and user

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.760
authorization, so
we would have things

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.810
in here like OpenID Connect,
social logins, other logins

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.530
where our partners can redirect
that user back to our identity

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.692
management solutions so
that they can sign in,

00:08:12.692 --> 00:08:14.400
and then we know that
it is actually them

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.900
without the partner
ever actually

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.000
seeing those credentials.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.829
Now, what if we start to get
into some really tricky areas?

00:08:21.829 --> 00:08:23.620
What if we're going to
expose patient data?

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.030
We're going to give information
about their medical history.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.064
What do we have to
think about then?

00:08:29.064 --> 00:08:30.730
Well, now, we have
to think about making

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.521
sure we're not end up
in jail because we're

00:08:32.521 --> 00:08:34.130
breaking a bunch of laws.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:35.770
So there is a time
and a place that we

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.510
need to have very,
very locked down,

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.340
tight security in our APIs.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:40.840
The point of this
presentation isn't

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.420
to say that we absolutely don't
need to think about tight API

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:44.920
security.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.400
We do, and there are times when
that's absolutely appropriate.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.635
So we want to make sure that
the person who's looking

00:08:50.635 --> 00:08:51.760
at this data is authorized.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.800
Is it your primary
care physician

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.020
that should have access
to this information,

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:57.910
or is it somebody from
marketing purposes

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.160
that wants to sell you a drug?

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.260
They probably shouldn't have
access to that information.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.010
We want to be able to
have an audit trail.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.801
We want to be able to
look back and see who

00:09:05.801 --> 00:09:07.674
has looked at this over time.

00:09:07.674 --> 00:09:09.840
At one of the hospitals
that I've worked with before

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.221
have had issues where
celebrities have come in,

00:09:12.221 --> 00:09:14.720
and people have wanted to kind
of see what's going on there.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:16.261
Obviously not
something that you want

00:09:16.261 --> 00:09:19.655
to have happen in a HIPAA
compliant environment.

00:09:19.655 --> 00:09:21.530
And then there are all
the basic things, too.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.363
Of course, we need to
still think about TLS.

00:09:23.363 --> 00:09:25.129
We need to use HTTPS.

00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:27.170
We're going to want to
look at payload encryption

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:29.544
in certain cases to make sure
that our data is very, very

00:09:29.544 --> 00:09:31.160
tightly locked down.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.140
And so from a
general perspective,

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.473
these are some of the things
that we need to think about

00:09:35.473 --> 00:09:36.640
from an API security--

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.010
as we're designing
our API security.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.880
And with that, I'm going to
hand it over to my colleague

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:48.950
Greg Brail, who is going to talk
about a few specific use cases

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:51.080
and case studies that
we've seen in the wild.

00:09:53.584 --> 00:09:54.500
GREG BRAIL: Excellent.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.345
Thanks Chris.

00:09:56.345 --> 00:09:57.970
So I'm going to talk
now a little bit--

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.070
Chris has talked
about the context.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.860
Why do we want to select
different types of API

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:03.370
security?

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.270
When is it important to
have everything locked down

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.752
to the max versus making
things flexible developers?

00:10:10.752 --> 00:10:12.460
But what I really want
to also talk about

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.910
is get into a little more detail
about API security and ways

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:17.080
to secure APIs.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.960
And the reason why I think it's
important to talk about this--

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:22.000
if you're a web
developer or have even

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.220
come close to web
development, and you

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.470
work for any sort of
moderately large company,

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:29.190
you probably had to go through
some sort of mandatory training

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.390
where they taught you about all
the things that can go wrong

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:32.590
with web app development.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.890
And you learned about CSS
attacks and SQL injection

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.110
and all kinds of
things like that.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.840
We don't have that for
APIs, and APIs are a little

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:41.981
different than web apps.

00:10:41.981 --> 00:10:44.230
There are some things that
can happen to web apps that

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:45.556
don't happen to APIs.

00:10:45.556 --> 00:10:46.930
There are some
things that happen

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:48.730
to APIs that you don't
think about as much

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.140
when you're building a web app.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.480
So I wanted to make sure we
talk a little bit about API

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.760
security, and one of
the ways I like to do it

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:57.190
is by talking a little bit
about some of the things that

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:58.990
can go badly when
you don't think

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:02.600
about all the aspects of API
security that are important.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.560
So for instance, if you remember
nothing else, everything

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:06.977
that has a URL--

00:11:06.977 --> 00:11:08.560
or if I'm really
fancy, I'll say URI--

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:10.150
has an API.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:14.320
So Chris said earlier, one
way to make an API secure

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.030
is not to have one.

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:19.480
That's true, but if you built a
mobile app, and the mobile app

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.050
talks to some service on the
internet, you have an API.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:25.300
You might not have
advertised it, developers.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.100
You might not have a
Swagger spec for it.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:29.560
You might not have GitHub
repo with sample code.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:32.650
But you have an API, and
a reasonably smart person

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.980
can, at home, jiggy
up their Wi-Fi router

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:40.090
to see what's going on
between your app and your API

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:41.930
and figure out what's happening.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.200
And a bunch of smart
people have done this

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.030
to embarrassing effect.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:49.810
For instance, the highly
critical enterprise API

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.000
run by Kylie Jenner
was broken to

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.860
by a 19-year-old
who looked at it

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:57.190
and discovered that his Kylie
Jenner mobile app, or actually

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.320
her website, had
no API security,

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:01.606
and he could get some
interesting information.

00:12:01.606 --> 00:12:02.980
Similarly, here's
a great article

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:04.750
I found on Programmable
Web that came out

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.500
about a year ago titled "How to
Use Undocumented APIs to Hack

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.210
Your Tesla."

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.060
That's something that, if you
own a Tesla, it's kind of cool.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.500
Hey, I can hack my Tesla.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.650
But the truth is that these
are all cases of when people

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.890
actually were able to look
in and see what was happening

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:24.250
between the client and the
server and figure out the API.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:27.280
So when we ignore the
fundamentals of API security

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.650
because we don't
have an API, you

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:32.680
have to really ask yourself,
well, do we have an API?

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:37.180
Maybe if everything is inside
one room or one LAN, OK,

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:38.260
we don't have an API.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.050
But otherwise, we do, and
as enterprise networks

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:45.130
get larger, as companies
get larger, as things like--

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.110
as companies have
global networks,

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:50.590
everything is effectively
an open network nowadays,

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.010
so you have to assume that
when you design your apps.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:58.210
So why are APIs important, and
why is API security important?

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.550
Well, a really great example
I can talk about here

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:03.010
is the world of banking.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:04.960
Especially in the
US, a lot of people

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.330
use services like
Mint or Yodlee,

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:10.840
which will do aggregation of
their bank account information.

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:11.800
I use Quicken a lot.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:13.270
It has a similar feature.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.920
And if you use any
of those services,

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:18.730
the first thing they do was
say great, give me your bank

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.830
account username and password.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:24.910
I should have used one of
Chris's disturbed person images

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.550
for that one because, once
someone says give me your bank

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.265
account username
and password, it

00:13:29.265 --> 00:13:30.640
means what they're
going to do is

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.800
they're going to store
your bank account password.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.270
Yeah, they're going
to encrypt it,

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.740
but they've got to decrypt
it in order to use it,

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:39.400
and then they're going to screen
scrape your bank's website.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:40.930
They're going to
make HTTP requests

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:42.607
as if they were a web browser.

00:13:42.607 --> 00:13:44.440
They're going to parse
the HTML, and they're

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:46.660
going to figure out what
your bank is telling them.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.310
So basically, some
of the banks have

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:51.400
started to realize that this
is not great for security

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.500
because when their
end users are giving

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:55.164
their passwords to
these third parties,

00:13:55.164 --> 00:13:57.580
you don't know what the third
parties are doing with them.

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:59.538
You don't know if the
third parties are storing

00:13:59.538 --> 00:14:01.240
that data in a safe way.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.460
Even if they're storing
that data in the safest way

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.790
possible, they still have
access to their end users'

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.910
unencrypted passwords, and
those passwords give you

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:12.690
access to do everything
you can do from your bank's

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:15.940
website, including transferring
large sums of money,

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:18.880
making big stock trades,
whatever you can do.

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:21.760
If we had took an API
approach, on the other hand,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:23.450
where banks were
to offer APIs that

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:27.010
used a security mechanism such
as OAuth, that would mean that,

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.260
as an end user, you would
not give these third parties

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:30.280
your password.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:32.620
You would instead go through
an authentication process

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:35.350
with the bank that gives
the third party token,

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:37.420
and that token can now
be set up by the bank

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.710
to only offer access to
these specific things

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:44.440
that the bank wants
them to have access to.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.540
So for instance, JP
Morgan Chase realized this

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:48.620
and wrote something in
their annual report,

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:49.810
and they got into a
little bit of trouble

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.490
with the "New York
Times" that said

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.130
[MOCKING] Jamie Dimon wants to
protect you from innovation.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:55.505
[NORMAL TONE] And
he has a point,

00:14:55.505 --> 00:14:56.680
and they have a point, too.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:02.260
But the idea here is by offering
open APIs with well-known API

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.030
security techniques
such as OAuth,

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:07.150
we can have a much, much
better handle on security

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.460
because security is not
about eliminating all risk.

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:11.590
Security is about managing risk.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.150
And by giving
third parties a way

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:15.070
to have access to
important information

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:18.520
like financial information
in a controlled way,

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.550
where you're only allowed to do
what the bank allows you to do,

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:23.980
we're in a much better
position than we are today,

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:25.810
where you give
someone your password,

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.970
and they can do anything.

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:30.720
So I'm now going to
talk about five or six

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:34.740
specific API security things
and kind of use some examples.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.290
So one big API security
mistake we can make

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.740
is to not use TLS, or SSL
as it used to be called,

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:42.540
and now the protocol
we're supposed to use

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.460
today is actually called TLS.

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:46.170
So for instance, a
security researcher

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.960
did a study last year,
and he discovered that,

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:52.140
around the world,
especially in South Asia,

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:56.910
at the time, something like
30% used non encrypted links.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.680
Two years ago, that was 90%.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.510
Now, maybe that's
not your password,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.850
but we're to the point
now on the internet where

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.730
everything that is even
slightly important, and in fact,

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:09.270
even everything that isn't,
should be encrypted with TLS.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:12.330
That's why google.com, when
you do a Google search,

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:13.950
is encrypted with TLS.

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:15.810
The cost is just not there.

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.930
But it's more than just,
hey remember to use TLS,

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.210
don't put an unencrypted
API on the internet.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.310
It's how you use TLS.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:26.010
So for instance, in some
frameworks for the client,

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:28.536
it's very easy to turn
off TLS verification

00:16:28.536 --> 00:16:29.910
because you're
doing development,

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.280
and it's kind of a pain to
get the right certificate,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.150
and the name on the DNS record
doesn't match the certificate,

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:37.440
and we're in a hurry.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:40.710
So we'll just check that
box in Node.JS or PHP

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.840
or Java or whatever
that says don't

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:45.000
verify the TLS certificate.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.240
That opens it up
for your client app

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.060
to be able to have its
supposedly secure web traffic

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:53.250
be intercepted by
anyone in Starbucks

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:56.350
who has control over the
Wi-Fi or over the DNS router.

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:59.100
TLS verification is
tremendously important.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:02.014
Not only that, TLS has
a bazillion options.

00:17:02.014 --> 00:17:03.180
What version are you using--

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.450
1.0, 1.1, 1.2?

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:07.010
What cipher suite are you using?

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:08.579
Are you using Elliptic Curve?

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:10.470
What encryption
algorithm are you using?

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.730
And these things
change all the time.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.480
So as anybody who's offering
an API or any web app

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:17.280
and is using TLS,
you have to remember

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.040
to keep up with what's
happening in the security world,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.380
follow the best practices
from your security

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:25.050
people and your company and
elsewhere, and make sure

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.420
that you're continually
evaluating how you're

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.300
using this technology and
keeping up with the latest

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:31.590
attacks.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.952
For instance, I still
see developers sometimes

00:17:34.952 --> 00:17:36.660
occasionally want to
write code, and they

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.330
want to hash something,
and they use MD5.

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:43.300
MD5 has been a no-no in the
security world for years.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:45.600
In fact, at Google, it's
not even in the code base.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.490
They took it out.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:50.410
But people still remember
this from a few years ago.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:52.710
So this is why it's very
important, anytime encryption

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:56.090
is used, to really
think about this.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.580
So now, we move on to some
of the other basics of APIs.

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:00.560
Chris talked about APIs
that require an end

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.330
user to be authenticated.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:05.090
There are all kinds of things
that can go wrong with this.

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:08.150
So for instance, the Nissan
LEAF a little while ago

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:10.550
came with a mobile
app, and the mobile app

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:12.050
let you do things
to your car like

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:13.550
turn on and off
the climate control

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:15.530
or start your car remotely.

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.900
And to make it easy for
people to use the app,

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.240
the authentication
mechanism for the app

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:21.830
required you to put
in the car's VIN

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:25.079
number, which is a unique
ID that's on every car.

00:18:25.079 --> 00:18:26.870
It's actually on the
dashboard of your car,

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:31.010
so it's not a secret, and it
is structured in such a way

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:34.220
that every type of car, most
of the digits are the same,

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:36.230
and only the last
five digits vary.

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:38.630
So you can imagine that
if you knew that, hey, I

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.580
can turn on the
climate control on one

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:43.610
Nissan LEAF using a VIN
number, what if I were

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:46.790
to cycle through 10
or 15,000 VIN numbers

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:50.110
and see if I can do that other
Nissan LEAFs in the world?

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:52.460
And a security researcher
named Troy Hunt figured out

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.229
that the answer was yes.

00:18:54.229 --> 00:18:56.270
Now, I'm not saying this
to make Nissan look bad,

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:57.770
because they fixed this.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:18:59.450
I'm saying this to
give you an example

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:00.949
of what happens
when you don't think

00:19:00.949 --> 00:19:03.140
through API authentication.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:04.640
There are a lot
of best practices

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:08.840
out there for basic things,
like signing users in, checking

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:11.600
passwords, resetting passwords.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.730
It's actually pretty
hard to get right.

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.800
Companies like Google
do a good job of it.

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:19.310
Smaller companies that are
specialized in the security

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.290
world do a good job of it.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.120
Large companies that
don't, don't always

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:24.120
do a great job of it.

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.880
I've seen some
horrible password reset

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.970
practices from various
websites I use in my life.

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:30.650
These kinds of
things can show up

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.590
as massive API security
vulnerabilities

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:35.740
if you're not careful.

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.950
But it's not just about
authenticating the end user.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:40.660
In the world of
APIs, one thing we

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.360
do that we did not do
in the world of web apps

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:45.850
is we also authenticate
the application.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:49.270
If you were to ask me what
does API management do--

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.280
because I am an
API management guy.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:53.080
That's what we do at Apigee.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:55.900
API management is about
managing the relationship

00:19:55.900 --> 00:20:00.010
between the developers who
build applications and the APIs

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:01.900
that they use, and
a big part of that

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.130
is managing those
security credentials.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:07.750
OAuth, which is the de facto
standard for API security now--

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:10.240
and in fact, from a
lot of security now--

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:13.810
has built into it the concept
of application credentials.

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.510
In order to build an application
that gets an OAuth token--

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:18.790
say they use a
Google Cloud API--

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:21.310
you not only have to supply
some user credentials,

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.650
but you've got a supply
application credentials, which

00:20:23.650 --> 00:20:26.800
the application developer gets
as part of the development

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:28.012
process.

00:20:28.012 --> 00:20:29.470
Now, the application
of credentials

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:33.220
are not 100% proof
against attacks.

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.770
I mean, anybody who has
access to the application

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:38.830
can use those application
credentials, But what they do

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:42.530
is they give the security team a
lot of extra layers of defense,

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:44.500
and that's what security
is really about.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:49.240
So for instance, we have had
cases of Apigee Edge customers

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:50.800
who had--

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.830
we had one who accidentally
pushed a version of an app

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:55.960
to the App Store
that had a bug in it.

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:58.660
And it was actually hitting
an authentication API

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:01.900
in an infinite loop,
hundreds of times a second,

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:05.770
and it was soon overwhelming
their whole technology stack.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:09.700
Now, if they had a different
set of application credentials

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:12.550
for every version and
platform of that app,

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:14.920
they actually would have
been able to cut that app off

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:17.590
instantly in their
API management tier,

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:19.780
or redirect traffic
from that app

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.474
to some sort of other server,
which is able to mediate it.

00:21:22.474 --> 00:21:24.640
And as a matter of fact,
they didn't quite did that,

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:26.650
but they did have the
application authenticated,

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:29.140
so they were able to
take some of those steps.

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:31.230
But that's an
example of why it's

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:32.980
important to not only
authenticate the end

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.660
user but the application.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:36.220
This also gives
you an opportunity

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:37.940
to do some things
like analytics,

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:40.570
and we'll talk a little bit
later about bot detection.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.630
But knowing which application
is making an API call

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.160
is one of the pieces
of information

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:48.530
you can use in that world.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:50.240
Now, I mentioned OAuth.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.280
OAuth has been around
for a couple of years.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:55.280
It's become the de facto
standard for API security.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:57.020
And basically, one
reason why it works

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:58.790
is because what it
allows you to do

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:02.450
is it allows a client who
makes an API call to exchange

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:05.870
some credentials for a token,
and that token gives you

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:07.550
access to the API.

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:10.670
And with OAuth, it's possible
for the API provider,

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:14.390
using software like Apigee or
many other bits of software,

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:17.240
to control what a
token is allowed to do.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:20.270
Furthermore, unlike a
password, a token uniquely

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:24.540
identifies a single
application on a single device.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:26.990
It's not just a generic bit
of security information,

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.240
and OAuth tokens
can be refreshed.

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:32.480
But OAuth is a fairly
complex family of specs.

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:33.760
They are IETF standards.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:36.385
There are a lot of smart people,
including some of our security

00:22:36.385 --> 00:22:38.120
folks at Google,
working on it, but there

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.050
are lots of ways to use OAuth.

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.680
So for instance,
one way to use OAuth

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.740
is to make an API call where
you send in your password

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:47.420
and get back the token.

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:51.410
Another way to do it is to have
the user's device open up a web

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:55.340
browser window where they can
get a token by being redirected

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:59.420
to a website, and that website
can do additional things

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:02.780
like check cookies, ask
for a second factor,

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.484
do all kinds of
things like that.

00:23:04.484 --> 00:23:06.650
Now, it turns out that the
OAuth folks have actually

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:09.170
been working on a spec
that describes, hey, these

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:12.470
are the best practices for how
to use OAuth in native app.

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.450
We recommend using this web
browser redirect method.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.179
Here's how to do it safely on
different mobile platforms.

00:23:19.179 --> 00:23:20.720
Furthermore, there
have been a couple

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:23.360
of things that have been found
as vulnerabilities in the OAuth

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:27.230
specs that have been fixed in
some of the newer IETF specs.

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:29.690
So it's really important, if
you're going to use OAuth,

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:31.580
to pay attention to
some of the requirements

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:34.121
that you see in the community
and some of the discussion that

00:23:34.121 --> 00:23:36.194
goes on, especially in
the IETF, because there

00:23:36.194 --> 00:23:37.610
are a lot of smart
people who have

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.026
written a lot of
really good stuff

00:23:39.026 --> 00:23:43.230
about how to use
OAuth correctly.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:44.730
I should also mention
authorization,

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:46.380
and in this form
of authorization,

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:48.210
I mean once you've
authenticated a user,

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.970
and you know who they are,
what are they allowed to use.

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:52.860
It's easy to make
some dumb mistakes.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.630
This one security
researcher discovered

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.109
that his bank actually--

00:23:57.109 --> 00:23:58.650
I don't think it
was a very big bank,

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:01.890
but they actually were not
verifying the account numbers

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.120
on the back end.

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:06.030
So he figured out the API
that his mobile app was using,

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.300
and he was able to transfer
money from any account

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:10.590
to any other
account in the bank.

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.690
Now, that's a huge mistake.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:15.060
That's like people
getting fired mistake,

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:17.190
but these kinds of
things can happen.

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.930
Developers don't always
think of these things.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:21.960
One of the things
I like about APIs

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:25.020
is that an API is a
contract, and it's

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.480
exactly clear what
is supposed to be

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.190
sent between the
client and the server.

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:32.820
So unlike a web app, where
you're downloading JavaScript

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:36.420
and executing who knows
what, a provider of an API

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:40.080
can specify exactly what's
sent, exactly what's received,

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.760
what the valid responses are.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.940
That means you can write test
plans and regression tests

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.250
and penetration
tests, and you can

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:49.860
verify that what you
think your API is,

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.750
is what you actually
implemented.

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:54.360
I think that's actually
an advantage of APIs,

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.520
and it's an advantage
of API security.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.610
And by having that kind of
spec, and by thoroughly testing

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:01.860
that spec, you should
be able to prevent

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:05.410
these kinds of dumb
authorization mistakes that--

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:07.830
they're easy to laugh
at now, but there

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:09.900
but for the grace
of God go all of us.

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:11.790
Right?

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:15.220
Another one, this is really
important, is rate limiting.

00:25:15.220 --> 00:25:18.270
So the best example of API
rate limiting I could find

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:20.850
came from a researcher that
did some work a couple of years

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:24.240
ago on Snapchat when they were
a much smaller and less mature

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:25.320
company.

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.032
He discovered that,
given a phone number,

00:25:27.032 --> 00:25:29.490
it was actually possible to
get information about someone's

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.200
Snapchat profile.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:33.030
Now, that doesn't sound
like a big problem,

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.970
but it turned out that there
was no rate limit on this API,

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:39.420
and he was able to send in
huge batches of phone numbers.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.570
Now, if you could imagine say
Beverly Hills, California--

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:44.040
I guess people use
cell phones now,

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.860
but even then, it doesn't have
that many phone numbers in it.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:48.690
It wouldn't take
very long to get

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:51.660
a lot of interesting
information that way.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:54.960
So this was, to me,
the best example of,

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.750
in the real world,
an API that should

00:25:57.750 --> 00:26:01.860
have had some sort of rate
limit to help with security.

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.980
And I mention this because
pretty much any API

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:07.830
that could be subject
to a brute force attack

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:09.960
is something that will be
subject to a brute force

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:10.830
attack.

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:13.110
And it may be subject
to a brute force attack

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:16.180
by someone who is just
hammering it for fun,

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:19.110
but it could be somebody
with legitimate credentials.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.990
Lots of people can sign up
for these services, so things

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:25.680
like product IDs,
user names-- if you

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:28.710
can put in someone's phone
number and get information.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.410
Another great example is
that OAuth password grant

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:34.140
type, where you send in your
password in an API call,

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.210
and you get back to
a security token.

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:38.130
Well, if there's no
rate limit on that,

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:40.800
now you have a distributed
password cracking API

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:44.070
that people can now use to
crack your password database.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.530
So it's great to monitor,
and this stuff is important.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.230
But by having basic
rate limits on APIs--

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:54.000
like no one should call this API
more than 500 times a second,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:57.060
or a given
application is limited

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:01.260
to a certain number of APIs
calls per day or hour or month,

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:04.260
or even possibly introducing
artificial delays

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:07.860
to some APIs that may be
called very frequently.

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.110
Those make a very,
very big difference

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:12.660
in the kind of security
posture you have and prevent

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:16.020
these kinds of attacks.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:20.430
And also we're going to finally
mention probably the biggest

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:22.989
security list of
things that developers

00:27:22.989 --> 00:27:24.530
need to know about,
comes from OWASP.

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:26.880
It's called the Top 10.

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.250
I couldn't find
anything since 2013,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:31.230
but basically, they
list the top threats

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:33.480
to applications on the internet.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:36.720
Interestingly enough, they
don't all apply to native apps

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:37.950
that use APIs.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:40.950
If you're building APIs,
some of the JavaScript things

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:44.020
and cross-site scripting things
don't really apply to you,

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:45.480
but the rest of it does.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:50.070
Injection attacks,
misconfiguration of security,

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:52.650
bad authorization decisions--
a lot of the things

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.520
that I've talked
about are in here,

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:56.770
but there are some things I
didn't talk about as well.

00:27:56.770 --> 00:27:58.260
For instance, the
number one attack

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:02.580
is an injection attack, so
classic SQL injection stuff,

00:28:02.580 --> 00:28:06.030
like can I put
semi-colon delete star

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:08.490
from customers in my user name.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:12.180
It applies to APIs just as
much as it applies to web apps.

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:14.610
Yes, there are things you can
do with regular expressions

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.130
and stuff to protect from
this, but it's really, really

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:18.960
important that, as
application developers,

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:22.200
we remain just as cognizant
of this in the world of APIs

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:26.260
as we do in the
world of web apps.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:27.760
So to sum up this
part, and then I'm

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.551
going to talk for two
or three more minutes

00:28:29.551 --> 00:28:30.850
and turn it over to Ismail.

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:33.820
I think everyone needs to know
some basic things about API

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:36.820
security, and this is not
necessarily the same things

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.220
you need to know for
web app security.

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:40.780
And a big part of
it, in my opinion,

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:42.488
is understanding the
importance of things

00:28:42.488 --> 00:28:47.020
like a rate limiting, traffic
management, content attacks,

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:49.510
and basically, the
idea that anything

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:52.390
that can be called
with a URI, someone

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:54.370
is going to figure out
what your app is doing

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:55.940
and try to break it.

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:59.320
And as app developers, we have
to be very cognizant of that.

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:02.010
At the same time,
by designing APIs

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:04.810
that follow the right
security principles, by making

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:08.890
smart choices about how much
security we need on an API.

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:12.280
And by making sure that our API
definitions are clear and well

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.170
understood, we've now
designed something

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:17.650
that we can test that has a much
smaller surface area than a web

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:18.480
app.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:20.920
And I would argue, if
you follow all the rules,

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.470
and you do everything right,
its possible to make things

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:26.560
with APIs that have fewer
security risks than what

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:30.330
we see today with web apps.

00:29:30.330 --> 00:29:33.401
Now, I'm going to talk for just
a short time about Apigee Edge.

00:29:33.401 --> 00:29:35.900
This is the product that has
been acquired by Google as part

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:38.960
of Google Cloud,
and basically, what

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:42.200
we do in Apigee is we
provide a product that

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:45.320
lets us insert ourselves
into the world of APIs.

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:49.040
So many companies, when they're
looking to offer an API,

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:51.320
they're asking themselves
how do I implement

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:52.370
a lot of these things?

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:55.320
How do I implement OAuth
and connect it to my users--

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.710
to my existing user
authentication system,

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.930
my existing single
signon system?

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:01.310
How do I implement a rate limit?

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:04.250
How do I do checks, basic
threat protection checks

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.124
like malformed JSON and XML.

00:30:06.124 --> 00:30:08.540
These are the kinds of things
that we've always been built

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:10.400
into the Apigee Edge product.

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:13.730
And the idea of Edge is
that, as an API provider,

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.460
you can put the API
management product--

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:18.590
and there are a lot of
products in this category--

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:22.820
in front of your API, and it can
handle a lot of these aspects,

00:30:22.820 --> 00:30:25.160
so that your back end,
the stuff your developers

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:28.384
are building-- you don't have to
build this into every service.

00:30:28.384 --> 00:30:30.050
The larger the company
you are, the more

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.240
it makes sense to
have one place where

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:35.270
you apply many of
these security policies

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:38.360
as opposed to building them
into every single application.

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:41.450
Many large companies--
Google has an API management

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:43.040
for its own APIs.

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:44.710
All the big companies
that have APIs

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.660
have API management as
part of their software

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:49.910
stack at the edge
of the network,

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:52.760
somewhere between the load
balancers and firewalls

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:55.340
and the stuff that
actually runs the APIs.

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:57.620
What Apigee Edge is--
it's a product that

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:01.160
allows you to configure it
to be that API management

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:05.427
layer for the API and configure
it however you need it to work.

00:31:05.427 --> 00:31:07.010
Now, I'm going to
talk for two minutes

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:08.360
here about Apigee
Sense, and this

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:10.110
is the last thing I'm
going to talk about,

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:12.447
which is a thing that we've
used to extend Apigee.

00:31:12.447 --> 00:31:14.780
And this is going to segue
nicely to what Ismail's going

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:18.440
to talk about, is we're going
to talk a little bit about bots.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.200
So there are all kinds of bad
things that can happen to APIs.

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:23.630
People who are not
legitimate users

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.540
may try to hammer
your API, to crack it,

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:29.690
to brute force it
just to mess with you.

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:33.050
But what can also happen, which
is a little bit more subtle

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:35.870
and harder to deal with, is
when legitimate users who

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:39.220
have got legitimate application
and end user credentials

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:42.230
use your APIs in an
illegitimate way.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:43.640
This happens a
ton, for instance,

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:46.340
in the travel industry,
where, gee, every hotel

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:47.720
has the same prices.

00:31:47.720 --> 00:31:49.460
Well, it's because
somebody somewhere

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:53.210
is screen scraping or botting
the APIs of all the other hotel

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.670
websites and trying to book
a room in every single price

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.790
category to try and find
out what the prices are

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:00.560
and what the availability is.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.570
There's a lot of bots
that happen in retail.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:04.550
Ismail comes from Ticketmaster.

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:06.321
Some of you may have
used Ticketmaster.

00:32:06.321 --> 00:32:07.820
They have some
experience with bots,

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:09.950
as much as they're
able to tell us.

00:32:09.950 --> 00:32:13.280
So Apigee Sense is a product
that uses the fact that we

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:17.270
collect a lot of data in the
Apigee platform about the APIs

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:21.690
that we handle, and basically,
we can use that information--

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.320
it's information about
IP addresses and things--

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:26.840
to figure out and to
score incoming API

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:30.200
calls on whether or not we
think they might be a bot.

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:33.470
Some of this is heuristic,
like for instance,

00:32:33.470 --> 00:32:36.470
American retail companies
don't have a lot of customers

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:37.850
in Eastern Europe.

00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:40.730
Or some of it is
heuristic like iPads

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.700
tend not to be located
in Amazon data centers.

00:32:43.700 --> 00:32:45.740
Some of it is machine
learning, and some of it

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:48.770
is standard big data stuff.

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:52.760
Was one OAuth token used
to make more APIs calls

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:56.140
than a human user could
have made using an app?

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.950
Was one OAuth tokin used
to scan through APIs,

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:02.450
like to scan through all the
phone numbers in Beverly Hills,

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:05.660
from 0000 to 99999?

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:08.240
These are the sorts of
things that you can see,

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:10.970
and by looking for
patterns like this,

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.370
we're able to then
score incoming API calls

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:16.580
as definitely a bot, like
we know this IP address is

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:19.490
a bot because everybody is
getting bot traffic from it,

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:22.190
to probably not a bot,
to something in between,

00:33:22.190 --> 00:33:23.930
and then take action.

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:26.960
Sometimes, take action
is block the API call,

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:30.080
but that lets the bot people
know that you're blocking them.

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:32.690
So sometimes a better action
is to let the back end know

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:35.340
so it can provide a different
result, a different price,

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:38.910
a different availability, even
slow down the API traffic.

00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:41.757
So that's kind of what
we're looking at doing.

00:33:41.757 --> 00:33:43.340
So with that, that's
really all that I

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:45.170
have to say about Apigee.

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:46.820
I'm now going to
turn it on to Ismail,

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:49.010
who is an SVP at
Ticketmaster and is going

00:33:49.010 --> 00:33:52.730
to talk to us about his
experiences with APIs and API

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:55.600
security.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:57.020
Thank you very much.

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:58.436
ISMAIL ELSHAREEF:
Thank you, guys.

00:33:58.436 --> 00:33:59.660
Oh, whoa, it's loud.

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.080
Hi, everyone.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:03.380
Thank you, Apigee and
Google, for having me here.

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:05.540
This is really
exciting to be here

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:08.900
to talk to you guys about
our experience with APIs

00:34:08.900 --> 00:34:10.219
from a business perspective.

00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:12.469
I'm going to talk a little
bit about the tech involved

00:34:12.469 --> 00:34:13.949
as well.

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:17.449
So at Ticketmaster, we're all
about delighting global fans

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:19.820
in a distributed world.

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:23.239
A little bit about myself
before I jump into that.

00:34:23.239 --> 00:34:27.739
I've been in this battlefield
of tech and product for a while

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:29.710
now.

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:33.712
I started as a software
engineer, back end engineer

00:34:33.712 --> 00:34:34.670
and front end engineer.

00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:37.310
I've been doing APIs
for the last seven

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:38.860
years or eight years.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:43.790
Joined Ticketmaster 18 months
ago to open up the platform

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:46.550
and open up their APIs,
and six months ago, I

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:49.850
started running the entire
product organization-- consumer

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:54.420
product organization,
so very, very exciting.

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:56.060
So show of hands--

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:59.960
who's been on Ticketmaster
the last six months?

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:01.400
Tried to buy tickets?

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:02.040
I can't see.

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:05.430
Not a lot, actually,
which is surprising.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:07.650
So keep your hands up.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:11.100
Keep it up if you were
not able to buy tickets

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.820
when you visited Ticketmaster.

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:15.406
I don't need this.

00:35:15.406 --> 00:35:16.280
I don't need to talk.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:16.790
I don't need to talk.

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:17.625
It's all good.

00:35:17.625 --> 00:35:18.250
It's happening.

00:35:18.250 --> 00:35:19.240
Oh, we have one person.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:20.660
OK, I'm here for you, man.

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:22.220
I'm here for you.

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:23.310
So we have problems.

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:26.570
Not every fan that comes
to Ticketmaster.com

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:28.650
could buy tickets.

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:30.260
So that's why, when
we started, what

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:33.200
really got me excited about
the opportunity at Ticketmaster

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:34.280
is the leadership there--

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:37.880
Jody Mulkey, the CTO,
and the entire team--

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:40.370
is that we're really
reinventing Ticketmaster.

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:43.130
We're changing the way
we're selling tickets,

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.250
and that's by having
a fan first thinking.

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:50.150
From our infrastructure,
and completely changing

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:51.770
and upgrading our
infrastructure--

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:55.100
we have an incredible
partnership with AWS and Amazon

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:58.460
where we're moving our
private cloud into AWS,

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:01.130
and it's a process that
we're working on right now--

00:36:01.130 --> 00:36:03.650
into opening our
APIs, and that's

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:07.760
a partnership that we have with
Apigee that's been fantastic.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:13.160
So infrastructure opening up
the APIs, and then our products

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:16.040
had to be built with
fans in mind first, which

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:19.340
is not traditionally the way
it's been done in the past.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:22.370
And what that means is that
really looking at all our fans,

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:23.960
different personas,
and understanding

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:27.530
why we're building the
products that we're building.

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:31.370
And our promise to our fans is
that we are unlocking moments

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:34.400
of joy for global fans
through unforgettable product

00:36:34.400 --> 00:36:35.150
experiences.

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:39.230
So if you think about that, and
thinking about the fan and fan

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:42.030
first, you'll see that--

00:36:42.030 --> 00:36:44.210
and I don't know if you
guys follow Ticketmaster--

00:36:44.210 --> 00:36:48.150
we have been changing the
way we offer value to fans.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:49.650
So for example,
this happened today.

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:52.730
It's a pre-sale for Ed Sheeran,
who's coming to North America.

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:55.460
It's going to be one of the
biggest tours this year.

00:36:55.460 --> 00:36:59.510
His new album dropped a week
ago, and it's killing it.

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:04.010
But this way is for us to
say, hey fans, preregister,

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:08.300
and we will text you a
link before the presale

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:11.390
where you can buy
Ed Sheeran tickets.

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:16.160
We did it with 1975, John Mayer,
Depeche Mode-- you name it.

00:37:16.160 --> 00:37:19.340
Because what we
find is that, when

00:37:19.340 --> 00:37:22.280
fans come to Ticketmaster for
Depeche Mode, for example,

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:22.790
who--

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:25.390
they haven't toured
in seven years,

00:37:25.390 --> 00:37:27.109
and if you can get
tickets, you're

00:37:27.109 --> 00:37:29.150
going to freak out because
I will freak out, too,

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:30.830
because I've been wanting to go.

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:32.960
And I signed up, and I got--

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:34.820
yes, we have a fan in the house.

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:36.080
I love it.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:37.640
But it's real.

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:41.660
No one's going to drop $4,000 to
go see Beyonce at the Rose Bowl

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:43.130
if they're thinking logically.

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:45.980
So it's an emotional
business that we're in.

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:49.640
We just want to make sure that
fans are not disappointed, are

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:52.820
not turned down, and
are actually getting

00:37:52.820 --> 00:37:54.740
the tickets that they want.

00:37:54.740 --> 00:38:00.410
We see 72 million
unique visitors a month.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.120
Not all of them are fans.

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:04.190
So we have a real,
real need here,

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:07.580
and a real problem to solve
with some of the challenges

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:08.570
we run into.

00:38:08.570 --> 00:38:11.270
So tickets are
the only commodity

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:15.140
I can think of that
go up in price--

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:17.300
up in value once you buy them.

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:21.430
I don't know if you guys bought
tickets to Hamilton recently.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:22.310
I have not seen it.

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:24.470
I can't afford it.

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:27.680
But we deal with a lot of
issues and a lot of problems.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:30.620
Bots are one of them.

00:38:30.620 --> 00:38:32.390
Limit violations--
people are trying

00:38:32.390 --> 00:38:35.180
to circumvent the limits
we put on an event.

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:37.040
So as a fan you cannot buy--

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:39.620
for certain events, you cannot
buy more than eight tickets.

00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:41.960
For some events, you cannot
buy more than four tickets,

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:45.890
just to make it fair and give
access to a lot of other fans

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:47.990
that want to go see the act.

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:50.300
And some people and some
bots and some bad actors

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:53.040
trying to circumvent that by
they want to get, I don't know,

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:54.500
100 tickets.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:57.770
I'm talking about concerts
now, and theater, not sporting

00:38:57.770 --> 00:38:59.360
events per se.

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:03.312
So there's that level of
complication and challenge

00:39:03.312 --> 00:39:05.270
that we have, and then
the biggest challenge we

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:07.820
have is reserve and do not buy.

00:39:07.820 --> 00:39:11.255
So during an on
sale, when people

00:39:11.255 --> 00:39:15.740
bad actors come and select
a pair of tickets or a bunch

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:17.600
of tickets, and they
don't actually end up

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:20.180
buying them, what happens
is that when they actually

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:25.790
select them, those tickets
are put into a hold,

00:39:25.790 --> 00:39:29.390
and no other fans can get
to them, which is bad.

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:32.450
We have ways of going
around that, obviously.

00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:34.140
We've been doing
this for a while now,

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:38.600
so we have our own algorithms
and our own systems that

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:43.520
can kind of determine or try to
glean whether this traffic is

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:47.330
coming from a good actor, a real
fan, a bad fan, a robot, a non

00:39:47.330 --> 00:39:50.240
robot, and all of
that good stuff.

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:52.430
But that's just
ticketmaster.com,

00:39:52.430 --> 00:39:53.180
Ticketmaster apps.

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:55.320
That's just our closed system.

00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:57.900
But in order for us to
deliver on our promise

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:01.250
to our fans, our global
fans, we determined that we

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:03.290
have to open up the platform.

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:06.680
We have to make our APIs
available to other developers,

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.260
to other partners,
clients, where

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:10.850
they can build
applications or can

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:13.170
build experiences
for their own fans,

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:16.200
so everyone gets access
to those tickets.

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:19.710
So when I started 18
months ago, what we did

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:23.390
was we consolidated all of
these different platforms

00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:26.420
that we have into one
simplified, very easy

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:30.820
to use platform partnered with
Apigee to expose those APIs.

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:32.480
And they would start
using those APIs

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:34.790
across the board in all
different channels starting

00:40:34.790 --> 00:40:37.280
with our own apps and
their own properties,

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:40.860
but also for pre-sales
on fan clubs,

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:44.660
for example, last minute
channels, you name it.

00:40:44.660 --> 00:40:48.980
So we have different
outlets for those APIs.

00:40:48.980 --> 00:40:50.780
But the reason we
wanted to go this route,

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:52.940
obviously to open up
the platform, but also

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:56.150
for any measurement of security
that we apply to one channel

00:40:56.150 --> 00:40:58.820
now, we apply it to the
APIs, and that's it.

00:40:58.820 --> 00:41:01.497
We don't have to think
about it anymore.

00:41:01.497 --> 00:41:03.080
Because when you
apply it to the APIs,

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.663
it doesn't matter
who's using the API.

00:41:04.663 --> 00:41:08.510
The same level of security
is going to be applied.

00:41:08.510 --> 00:41:11.660
In our world, what Greg
said, that security

00:41:11.660 --> 00:41:12.920
is not eliminating risk.

00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:14.660
It's mitigating risk,
and for us, it's

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:16.579
all about mitigating risk.

00:41:16.579 --> 00:41:17.870
It's all about mitigating risk.

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:20.840
We'll never know, in real
time, who's a true fan

00:41:20.840 --> 00:41:22.100
and who's a bot.

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:25.940
So if you're like me, if you
go to a show once a week--

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:27.440
and I pay out of
pocket, by the way.

00:41:27.440 --> 00:41:28.740
I don't get free tickets.

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:32.570
If you know how to get
free tickets, let me know.

00:41:32.570 --> 00:41:35.630
My behavior and my
pattern of activity

00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:37.970
can look like a
bot to our system

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:43.160
because I'm coming from
my home wifi trying

00:41:43.160 --> 00:41:47.300
to buy tickets to John Mayer,
to 1975, this and that.

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:49.610
And it's like, oh, my god,
this guy or that entity

00:41:49.610 --> 00:41:53.300
is buying a lot of tickets,
so maybe it's suspect.

00:41:53.300 --> 00:41:55.370
We're trying to make
it smarter and better,

00:41:55.370 --> 00:42:00.080
but true fans and super fans
can come across as bots,

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:03.950
and that's a problem that
we're trying to solve.

00:42:03.950 --> 00:42:05.900
But we opened up the platform.

00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:09.830
We have a portal,
developer.ticketmaster.com.

00:42:09.830 --> 00:42:13.850
We created an ecosystem where
you can go to the portal

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:17.302
and understand what APIs
we have, who's using them,

00:42:17.302 --> 00:42:18.760
and how you can
get access to them.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:22.670
We really followed all the
best practices and everything

00:42:22.670 --> 00:42:27.410
that Apigee advocates
and puts out there.

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:30.830
And from my previous experience
as well, all the best

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:34.310
practices that I learned
the hard way sometimes,

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:36.890
that we should be
doing in order for us

00:42:36.890 --> 00:42:40.280
to have a successful
API program.

00:42:40.280 --> 00:42:42.850
So we've done all of this stuff.

00:42:42.850 --> 00:42:45.880
We integrated with some
incredible partners

00:42:45.880 --> 00:42:50.320
to get access and to reach other
fans that might not necessarily

00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:52.120
come to ticketmaster.com.

00:42:52.120 --> 00:42:56.590
And as a result of that
opening up the platform

00:42:56.590 --> 00:42:59.800
and really thinking
broadly, we've seen,

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:04.660
just last year, that 10%
of all tickets sold by us

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:06.940
came off of platform,
came through partners,

00:43:06.940 --> 00:43:12.220
came through our APIs,
outside of our products.

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:13.630
Fascinating, right?

00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:16.570
But guess what-- the
challenges still remain.

00:43:16.570 --> 00:43:20.950
The challenges that we've
seen in our own products

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:25.470
still remained true in
the open platform world.

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:28.570
They just got amplified.

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:31.990
Actually, almost
all the errors we

00:43:31.990 --> 00:43:35.470
see in the Apigee portal
coming through our APIs

00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:38.740
are of these two
kinds of errors.

00:43:38.740 --> 00:43:41.230
Too many requests, rate
limiting-- so people

00:43:41.230 --> 00:43:43.240
trying to make too
many API requests,

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:45.880
trying to get access to
those tickets, reserve them.

00:43:45.880 --> 00:43:47.830
And because we
have rate limiting,

00:43:47.830 --> 00:43:51.400
it's preventing our system from
going down and overwhelming

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:54.070
our system, but also
really controlling

00:43:54.070 --> 00:43:58.510
the API access for that
particular user or application.

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:02.154
But also look at the
unauthorized calls.

00:44:02.154 --> 00:44:03.820
A lot of people are
trying to make calls

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:07.780
without being authorized,
without the right OAuth token.

00:44:07.780 --> 00:44:10.300
It's a real problem.

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:11.890
And because building an API--

00:44:11.890 --> 00:44:15.760
a lot of people think, oh,
it's a simple interface,

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:17.669
and I have the SDK
and documentation.

00:44:17.669 --> 00:44:18.460
It's pretty simple.

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:19.210
Right?

00:44:19.210 --> 00:44:21.970
It actually takes a lot
of effort and planning,

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:24.640
especially when it comes to
security, identity management,

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:28.210
who has access to what, error
messaging, all of that--

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:31.740
it takes a village for the
API to actually be useful

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:34.690
and to be simple.

00:44:34.690 --> 00:44:37.540
That's the challenge
we run into,

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:42.220
so when we focus on the fan
and opening up everything

00:44:42.220 --> 00:44:50.080
and trying to think how do we
make that access of this API

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:53.530
that we just put out
there favorable to fans,

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:56.020
and for developers to build
applications that actually

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:58.160
let fans through.

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:02.050
So the way we did it was
really follow the basic best

00:45:02.050 --> 00:45:02.860
practices.

00:45:02.860 --> 00:45:04.150
I cannot emphasize that.

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:08.080
A lot of times, the
biggest problems

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:11.380
happen because you don't follow
the guidelines and the best

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:14.650
practices, like authorization,
authentication, OAuth,

00:45:14.650 --> 00:45:18.640
rate limiting, using bot
blocking that Apigee provide

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:22.790
if you don't do this
for a living like we do,

00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:24.929
SSL, just the basic stuff.

00:45:24.929 --> 00:45:26.470
And if you do the
basic stuff, you're

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:31.000
actually 50% there, like Greg
and Chris were talking about.

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:36.010
In our case, we really had to
think about the reserve API

00:45:36.010 --> 00:45:37.240
call.

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:41.110
That's where actually people
put real tickets, real value,

00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:45.921
on hold, so we need to rethink
how that API call actually

00:45:45.921 --> 00:45:46.420
works.

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:49.982
So the way we did it was
we're introducing friction.

00:45:49.982 --> 00:45:51.440
We're trying to
introduce friction.

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:53.810
It's like you cannot
do that anonymously.

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:55.720
You authenticated
your application.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:56.830
We have the OAuth token.

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:57.520
Great.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:46:00.340
But now, we want to know
who's the actual user making

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:04.030
that call, because
then I'll have

00:46:04.030 --> 00:46:07.360
extra information about
that user that will help me

00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:11.830
determine is that
really a legitimate fan,

00:46:11.830 --> 00:46:14.125
or is that a bot
that's trying to--

00:46:14.125 --> 00:46:19.090
that hacked the app, and
now using it in a bad way.

00:46:19.090 --> 00:46:23.800
So introducing friction
by forcing registration

00:46:23.800 --> 00:46:27.460
or signing in, using whatever
you want to sign in for.

00:46:27.460 --> 00:46:31.390
We use Ticketmaster
Accounts for that.

00:46:31.390 --> 00:46:34.210
And using the fan score,
which is our algorithm

00:46:34.210 --> 00:46:37.510
to determine whether you're
a good actor or a bad actor--

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:40.330
basically, a fan or a bot.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:41.980
And the last thing,
obviously, which

00:46:41.980 --> 00:46:43.760
is really, really important--

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:45.970
and we just did
that with Apigee--

00:46:45.970 --> 00:46:48.520
is identity management,
entitlements.

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:52.550
So when somebody's making an API
call with their OAuth token--

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:55.420
so if that checks out,
that means Apigee says,

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:57.310
OK, this OAuth token is good.

00:46:57.310 --> 00:47:00.010
Now, taking their API
key and their identity

00:47:00.010 --> 00:47:05.890
and pinging our system, saying
what role does this player

00:47:05.890 --> 00:47:07.360
have in our system?

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:08.500
Are they a supervisor?

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:11.290
Are they a fan with a bunch of
tickets that they can transfer,

00:47:11.290 --> 00:47:12.251
and all of that stuff?

00:47:12.251 --> 00:47:14.500
And once you get that back,
you add a bunch of headers

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:18.070
to the call, and then when
we get it in the back end,

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:24.290
we understand if this action is
permitted by this user or not.

00:47:24.290 --> 00:47:26.200
So if you want to
transfer some tickets--

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:29.980
now just because you're a
fan doesn't mean anything.

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:32.410
You have to be a
fan and also have

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:34.990
the rights to
transfer those tickets

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:37.910
or to sell those tickets
or to cancel an order.

00:47:37.910 --> 00:47:40.390
So these are the three
learnings that we found.

00:47:40.390 --> 00:47:44.620
Just opening up an API ecosystem
and a platform actually

00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:45.820
works really well.

00:47:45.820 --> 00:47:46.600
Is it perfect?

00:47:46.600 --> 00:47:47.180
It's not.

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:49.180
It's never going to be
perfect, and we're always

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:51.460
learning every single day.

00:47:51.460 --> 00:47:55.270
But the fact that we
sold 10% of our tickets

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:58.840
off of platform using our
APIs that we opened up

00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:03.310
just 18 months ago is a huge
testament to the power of APIs

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:07.180
to business, but also
the fact that you

00:48:07.180 --> 00:48:10.060
have to stay vigilant in order
for you to really expand.

00:48:10.060 --> 00:48:14.260
I mean, we haven't scratched
the surface with APIs just yet.

00:48:14.260 --> 00:48:18.310
And with that, I thank you for
being here, and we're hiring.

00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:21.250
We want people to help us
with this transformation

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:21.970
that we're on.

00:48:21.970 --> 00:48:25.630
And I guess I'll
open up to questions.

00:48:25.630 --> 00:48:28.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

